  "query_preview": "..\n\nLittle in the way of impactful weather is expected for the rest of \nthis week into next week. An upper level low pressure system will \nbecome cut off from the Polar Jet and sit off of the WA coast \nthrough Saturday. The low then deepens and digs as it becomes \nentrained in an active Sub-Tropical Jet moving across California \ninto the Great Basin. The digging motion will divert much of the \nenergy and dynamics associated with the low south of the Inland\nNorthwest as the trough ejects across the Northwest. Ridging of\nhigh pressure then looks to establish itself over the the \nregion by early next week. Cluster analysis of the model \nensembles suggests high confidence for a strong ridge to \ndominate the weather pattern for at least Monday and Tuesday. \nThe general consensus indicates upper level energy undercutting \nthe ridge on Wednesday with active weather impacting California.\nIt won't be until the next shortwave disturbance around \nWednesday night into Thursday that the ridge displaces to the \neast, and this looks to be when we will look to see \nprecipitation chances increasing again over the Inland \nNorthwest. This far out in the forecast carries low confidence \nand even fringe scenarios indicate little more than minor \nimpacts in the far extended.\n\nRest of Thursday through Sunday: Low pressure spinning off of the WA \ncoast will promote easterly flow at low levels. The east slopes of \nthe Cascades will see weak orographic lift tonight and will be \nsupported with a weak warm front that pushes in over the Cascades by \nFriday morning. Both of these lifting mechanisms will be weak and \nmoisture will be limited resulting in a 50-70% chance for light \nprecipitation. Snow levels will start out around 2500-3000 feet and \nrise to around 4000-4500 feet by the afternoon. At most, looking at \nsnow accumulations of up to an 1 inch for travel over Stevens Pass \nand Washington Pass Friday morning into Friday afternoon.\n\nThe longwave trough of low pressure ejecting across Saturday into \nSunday night will favor precipitation over southeast WA and the \nsouthern ID Panhandle. Snow levels will have climbed above mountain \npass level by then, and probabilities for measurable rainfall is \nonly 20-40% for Spokane to Lookout Pass and down to Pullman. \nProbabilities increase to around 50-60% on the Camas Prairie. \nChances for rain will be increasing through the evening for Trick-or-\nTreaters, but overall doesn't look to be a wet Halloween evening. \nTotal rainfall will generally be around an tenth of an inch or less \nand will bring little to no impacts.\n\nTemperatures will be near normal through the rest of the week with \nhighs in the upper 40s to low 50s and lows generally in the upper \n20s to low 30s.\n\nSunday night through Thursday: Next week looks even more benign. \nSubsidence under the rideg of high pressure to begin the week \nwill suppress potential for precipitation. The ridge does look \n\"dirty\" with high clouds passing over the top and across the\nNorthwest region. The boundary layer will remain moist under \nthe ridge and that typically means fog and stratus cover is \nexpected. The prospects of high clouds makes forecasting fog and\nstratus more challenging as that will act to inhibit \nradiational cooling a bit. Regardless, this ridge doesn't look \nto be a big concern for producing stagnant conditions that would\nnecessitate an Air Stagnation Advisory. If strong inversions do\ndevelop, then that would most likely result in a lot of stratus\nforming and we tend to mix a bit better each day under stratus \ncover. The ridge also may not be in place long enough to meet \nthe time requirements of 72 hours of stagnant air for an \nadvisory as well. Model ensembles are in general agreement for \nthe ridge to be displaced around Wednesday night into Thursday. \nPotential for precipitation is low though as the ridge displaces\nas the prognosis looks more favorable for energy to dig south \nof the region. Temperatures will see little change from this \nweekend through next week. /SVH",
